# Disjoint intramolecular ring closure within a finite A-B complex —
# the Phase-3 counterpart of the TLBR ring-closure rule, but with a bounded
# (non-gelling) reaction network so BNG2 can enumerate an exact oracle.
#
# Two bivalent molecules A(p,q), B(p,q).  A p-p bond joins them into a dimer;
# a q-q bond then closes intramolecularly into a two-bond ring.  No free sites
# remain once both bonds form and cross-linking is impossible (a dimer has no
# free p), so the network is FINITE: exactly four complex species —
#     A(p,q),  B(p,q),  A(p!1,q).B(p!1,q),  A(p!1,q!2).B(p!1,q!2).
#
#   bind:    A(p) + B(p)         ->^kf1  A(p!1).B(p!1)      (bimolecular assoc)
#   unbind:  A(p!1).B(p!1)       ->^kr1  A(p) + B(p)        (dissociation)
#   close:   A(q).B(q)           ->^kf2  A(q!1).B(q!1)      (DISJOINT ring
#                                                            closure, needs
#                                                            complex expansion)
#   open:    A(q!1).B(q!1)       ->^kr2  A(q).B(q)          (ring opening — the
#                                                            A-B pair stays
#                                                            connected via the
#                                                            p-bond)
#
# `close` is the Phase-3 disjoint multi-molecule ring closure (`A(q).B(q)` is a
# disconnected reactant pattern → the two molecules must be in the SAME complex,
# i.e. already joined by the p-bond).  `open` is the ring-opening reverse: it
# breaks the q-bond but the A-B pair stays connected through the p-bond, so its
# product is the connected `A(q).B(q)` (n_product_patterns == 1, DeleteBond that
# keeps the complex whole).  Both fire only inside an existing A-B complex, so a
# scaled batch of either exercises the same-complex within-batch interaction the
# gate used to forbid.  `unbind`'s `+` product only separates a SINGLE-bonded
# dimer (a closed ring stays connected through the q-bond), so its
# product-molecularity check must run per sampled match in the batch.
#
# Well-mixed mass action.  Writing Af=Bf (free A/B), S (single p-bond dimer),
# D (closed ring), with A_tot=B_tot: conservation Af + S + D = A_tot, and
#   kf1*Af*Bf = kr1*S     (bind/unbind)
#   kf2*S     = kr2*D     (close/open).
# With A_tot=B_tot=600, kf1=0.01, kr1=2.0, kf2=1.0, kr2=1.0 the equilibrium is
#   Af = Bf = S = D = 200.
# The seed sits there, so the ensemble is stationary and any first-moment bias
# in a scaled batch (bind / unbind / close / open) drifts the mean.
#
# Minimum reactant population at equilibrium is 200 for every rule (free A/B for
# bind; S for unbind and close; D for open), so K = floor(200/Nc):
# Nc=20 -> K=10, Nc=50 -> K=4, Nc=100 -> K=2.
#
# Oracle for the scaled (Nc > 0) path: the analytic equilibrium (all 200), the
# exact-RM ensemble, and BNG2 (ODE/SSA over the finite network).  NFsim has no
# partial scaling and is NOT an oracle for it.

begin parameters
    kf1     0.01
    kr1     2.0
    kf2     1.0
    kr2     1.0
    Af_ss   200      # free A at equilibrium (= free B)
    Bf_ss   200
    S_ss    200      # single p-bond dimers
    D_ss    200      # closed rings (double bond)
end parameters

begin molecule types
    A(p,q)
    B(p,q)
end molecule types

begin seed species
    A(p,q)                     Af_ss
    B(p,q)                     Bf_ss
    A(p!1,q).B(p!1,q)          S_ss
    A(p!1,q!2).B(p!1,q!2)      D_ss
end seed species

begin observables
    Molecules  Afree    A(p,q)
    Molecules  Ssingle  A(p!1,q).B(p!1,q)
    Molecules  Dring    A(p!1,q!2).B(p!1,q!2)
end observables

begin reaction rules
    bind:    A(p) + B(p)          -> A(p!1).B(p!1)       kf1
    unbind:  A(p!1).B(p!1)        -> A(p) + B(p)         kr1
    close:   A(q).B(q)            -> A(q!1).B(q!1)       kf2
    open:    A(q!1).B(q!1)        -> A(q).B(q)           kr2
end reaction rules

begin actions
    simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>10,n_steps=>100})
end actions
